AB  - Software supply chain attacks increasingly target trusted development and delivery processes, making conventional post-build integrity mechanisms insufficient. Existing frameworks like SLSA, SBOM, and in-toto mainly provide provenance and traceability but cannot actively identify or remove vulnerabilities during production. This paper presents an agentic AI approach for autonomous software supply chain security, combining large language model (LLM) reasoning, reinforcement learning (RL), and multi-agent coordination. The system employs specialized security agents that interact with CI/CD environments, collaborate intelligently to identify and mitigate threats, and document actions for integrity and auditing. RL enables adaptive mitigation strategies balancing security effectiveness and operational overhead, while LLMs provide semantic vulnerability analysis and explainable decisions. The framework is evaluated on simulated pipelines and actual CI/CD integrations, including injection attacks, insecure deserialization, access control violations, and configuration errors. Results show improved detection accuracy, shorter mitigation latency, and reasonable build-time overhead compared to rule-based, provenance-only, and RL-only baselines, demonstrating that agentic AI can enable proactive, self-defending software supply chains.
ER  - 

