AB  - Fintech platforms such as payment gateways, digital banks, and trading systems increasingly adopt micro services and cloud-native architectures, which makes traditional monitoring approaches insufficient for ensuring reliability and regulatory compliance. This paper examines how observability-driven DevOps, powered by distributed tracing and AIOps, can improve reliability and incident response in Fintech systems. We focus on three contributions. First, we characterize observability requirements for Fintech workloads and describe an end-to-end reference architecture using Open Telemetry for vendor-neutral distributed tracing, logging, and metrics collection. Second, we propose an AIOps-based anomaly detection pipeline that leverages trace- and metric-level features to detect performance and reliability anomalies in CI/CD stages and in production runtime. Third, we relate these techniques to relevant standards and best practices, including the NIST AI Risk Management Framework, SRE error budget concepts, and emerging IEEE/ISO guidance on trustworthy AI and operational resilience. We illustrate the approach with TikZ-based architecture diagrams and a conceptual Fintech case study, and we discuss challenges around data volume, sampling, model drift, and regulatory expectations. Our analysis suggests that combining Open Telemetry-based observability with AIOps-style analytics can transform DevOps in Fintech from reactive firefighting into proactive, evidence-driven operations.
ER  - 

