AB  - Artificial intelligence (AI), DevOps techniques, quantum computing, and serverless architectures taken together offer hitherto unheard-of possibilities for retail system optimization. This work explores the possibilities and difficulties resulting from applying AI-driven DevOps techniques in quantum computing settings to improve serverless application performance in retail environments. By means of empirical analysis of hybrid quantum-classical deployment pipelines, we show that, under high availability for retail applications, AI-enhanced DevOps practices can attain up to 40% improvement in deployment efficiency and 35% reduction in quantum resource consumption. Important results show that combined with machine learning-based resource optimization, quantum-aware continuous integration/continuous deployment (CI/CD) pipelines greatly improve scalability and performance criteria. Evaluating three retail scenarios—dynamic pricing optimization, inventory management, and fraud detection—the research uses a mixed-methods approach combining controlled experiments with IBM Qiskit simulators and AWS Lambda serverless capabilities. Among all the scenarios, results show 51% deployment time reduction, 35% quantum resource optimization, and enhanced application performance. Challenges include, nevertheless, organizational skill gaps, hybrid system complexity, quantum decoherence management, and specialized toolchain needs. The paper offers a complete structure for companies trying to keep strong DevOps standards while using quantum computing benefits in retail serverless architectures. This study adds empirical data, useful recommendations, and performance criteria for real-world implementation scenarios, so supporting the developing field of quantum-enhanced enterprise systems.
ER  - 

