AB  - This article presents a hybrid approach to code analysis that combines static analysis, dynamic profiling, and machine learning methods to improve the efficiency of vulnerability and defect detection. The core idea of this approach is to intelligently prioritize code sections: computationally expensive dynamic analysis and directed greybox fuzzing methods are applied only to those functions that the machine learning model assesses as potentially risky. A single software function is considered as the unit of analysis. For each function, a set of static characteristics is generated, reflecting the structural complexity and interrelationships within the code, as well as dynamic characteristics describing runtime behavior. Based on these features, a Random Forest model is trained, which estimates the probability of a defect’s presence and is used to decide whether a more in-depth check is necessary. This approach allows us to significantly reduce the number of false positives typical of traditional static analysis, while maintaining a high level of defect detection. The paper compares static, dynamic, and hybrid analysis and discusses the benefits of using ensemble machine learning models to filter and rank results. Particular attention is given to scalability and integration of the proposed solution into existing software development processes, including CI/CD pipelines. It is shown that selective use of dynamic analysis ensures efficient use of computing resources and makes the approach applicable to large codebases. The method’s limitations related to the quality of training data, the completeness of dynamic coverage, and the interpretability of model decisions are also discussed. The presented approach demonstrates a balance between accuracy, performance and applicability for software safety analysis in industrial settings.
ER  - 

