AB  - Generative AI software like ChatGPT and Codex are revolutionising the development of banking tools by automatically providing new systems for flagging security vulnerabilities and detecting investments which are likely to be fraudulent. But these capabilities come with ethical and regulatory implications, such as potential for infringement of intellectual property, biased decision-making, opacity, and overreliance on automation, which may run afoul of critical banking regulations, such as anti money laundering (AML), know-your-customer (KYC), and fair lending laws. This paper discussesa big data-driven AI/ML-based framework to assess these risks by employing big codebases and advanced analytics. Four metrics—Plagiarism Risk Score (PRS), Bias Propagation Index (BPI), Explainability Gap (EG), and Automation Dependency Score (ADS)—are aggregated into a Composite Ethical Risk Score (ERS) using Multi-Criteria Decision Analysis (MCDA). Powered by Apache Spark and AI models like CodeBERT and BERT, the framework ensures scalability and regulatory alignment. A production-ready Google Colab implementation with synthetic data demonstrates applicability, while an open-source toolkit with Apache Superset dashboards enables real-time CI/CD integration, aligned with IEEE 7000 standards. This work bridges AI ethics and banking compliance, offering a scalable solution for responsible AI adoption. Our findings offer practical tools for AI governance in regulated domains through quantitative approach through novel metrics.
ER  - 

