AB  - There are still a number of major issues associated with the last mile of Machine Learning model production/deployment, or when it comes to Scaling-Up Production Environment (PPM). Most times, prototypes are in the sand-box phase, which makes them not able to properly transition into a stable, scalable production environment. This paper will suggest an MLOps Framework to provide a methodology (a set of Tools) for the operationalization of machine learning models and provide a focus for the operationalization of MLOps within the context of the Model Factory architecture pattern, thereby supporting Standardized, Reproducible, and Governed Lifecycle Management of ML. In addition, this paper will highlight other important prescriptions for Continuous Automation, which extend past the traditional CI/CD through Continuous Testing (CT) and Continuous Monitoring (CM). In addition to the primary contributions of this paper, the following items are included as part of the MLOps Framework: 1.Define implementation tiers as Code, Data, and Model, 2.Develop and implement An Interpretable Model Drift Detection and Application System that can work with Nonstationary Data Environments, and 3.Create a Well-Defined Governance Structure based on Model Versioning and Lineage Tracking for Auditable Accountability. The framework is intended to apply to diverse libraries-e.g., H2O or Scikit-Learn in supporting real enterprise-class systems with interfaces made standard across systems supported via centralized metadata management.
ER  - 

