AB  - The operational risks due to deployment failure in CI/CD pipelines are substantial, which can impact system availability, financial loss, and user experience. However, traditional validation methods based on individual signal validation and human judgment are not effective in coping with the increasing number of deployments in CI/CD pipelines. In this study, we proposed a machine learning-based predictive model that combines pull requests, build, and test signals to forecast deployment risks before software release. To train and validate our model, we utilized a data set containing 6,240 deployments and 15 engineered features in four different domains. Five different supervised learning algorithms were implemented to train our predictive model: logistic regression, decision trees, random forests, gradient boosting machines, and XGBoost. To ensure model robustness, stratified cross-validation was utilized. Our experimental results show that ensemble methods outperform traditional validation methods in deployment risk prediction. XGBoost was identified as the most accurate model with an accuracy of 97.80% and an AUC value of 0.986. The results also show that combining multiple domains can significantly improve model accuracy. In conclusion, our proposed model can contribute to the development of more intelligent deployment pipelines to support data-driven decisions in software systems.
ER  - 

