AB  - Large-scale software systems frequently encounter hidden scalability constraints, such as tightly coupled modules or non-parallelizable code paths that trigger bottlenecks under load. Existing techniques largely rely on static metrics or postdeployment profiling, detecting issues only after performance degradation occurs. To address this gap, we present ScalePulse, a lightweight, combinatorial large language model (LLM)-driven framework that continuously analyzes code and architectural artifacts to identify and explain emerging scalability risks in real time. The framework integrates four key components: PulseCore, a hybrid transformer with dynamic attention over dependency graphs and UML models to compute per-module constraint-risk scores; SurgeMesh, a propagation engine that models systemic risk using a modified PageRank algorithm; ScaleSync Forecaster, which predicts future bottlenecks via risktrend time-series modeling; and XAI-Scale Explainer, which applies SHAP and LIME to produce developer-centric justifications for each recommendation. Evaluations on a 50-module banking microservices platform and a large open-source web application demonstrate 92% precision, 89% recall, and up to 30% throughput improvement, with less than 2.5 seconds of runtime overhead per commit. By embedding explainable, predictive scalability assessment directly into IDEs, Git workflows, and CI/CD pipelines, ScalePulse enables proactive refactoring and makes scalable design a natural part of everyday software development.
ER  - 

