AB  - Static Application Security Testing (SAST) tools play a critical role in identifying vulnerabilities during early software development stages; however, their effectiveness is often limited by extremely high false positive rates (FPRs). This paper introduces a hybrid workflow that integrates a high-recall static analyzer, Semgrep, with Large Language Model (LLM)–based validation to improve detection precision while maintaining high recall. Using the OWASP Benchmark v1.2 dataset comprising 2,740 labeled Java test cases, we systematically evaluate multiple models, including GPT-5-mini, GPT5-nano, and Claude-haiku, against a Semgrep-only baseline. Experimental results show that the proposed LLM-augmented static analysis drastically reduces the false positive rate from 91.8% to 11.9%, achieving a state-of-the-art F1-score of 92.84% and recovering 57% of vulnerabilities missed by Semgrep. Moreover, the entire validation process completes in under 44 minutes at a total cost of $1.44, confirming the feasibility of this approach for CI/CD integration. A detailed per-CWE and interaction analysis reveals that LLM validation eliminates 88.7% of Semgrep’s false alarms with minimal risk of overruling true positives (≈1%), while also exposing consistent weaknesses in cryptographic CWE-328 (Weak Hash). The results establish a practical pathway toward semantic, high-precision vulnerability detection through cost-efficient LLM validation layers.
ER  - 

