AB  - Software delivery pipelines now need to be more secure, scalable, and robust due to the rapid use of cloud-native architectures. Security bottlenecks and lengthy release cycles are caused by traditional DevSecOps frameworks' high false positive rates, vulnerability to slow vulnerability alerts, and lack of automation. In order to solve these problems, this paper offers an AI-Integrated DevSecOps paradigm which integrates automation, intelligence, and continuous compliance into an already-existing CI/CD pipeline. The framework was developed through a design science research approach by combining reference architecture design, policy-as-code enforcement, artificial intelligence (AI) model integration and a controlled experiment. Fine-tuning of large language models for secure code review, risk-based vulnerability prioritisation based on machine learning, and a time-series anomaly detection model to track runtime telemetry were developed. Red team simulations and a production-scale cloud testbed with seeded controlled vulnerabilities were utilized to assess the system. With a mean time to detect of 4.5 minutes, a mean time to remediate of 12 minutes, a false positive rate of 3.2, and an F1-score of 97.5—much better than five state-of-the-art methods—the quantitative results are also encouraging. The findings demonstrate that the AI Integrated DevSecOps can provide software releases that are safe, quick, and dependable, which will aid in bridging the gap between security assurance and delivery agility after the tasks are carried out.
ER  - 

