AB  - Selenium-based automated web testing becomes brittle because even small modifications to web page elements and their corresponding locators result in repeated test breakdowns which need prolonged upkeep. Web applications face limitations in their self-healing capabilities because traditional rule-based strategies fail to adapt to changes in application structure. The research assesses five open-source Large Language Models (LLMs) which include Zephyr, Mistral, Falcon, LLaMA and Gemma to perform self-healing operations on Selenium tests through generative AI while evaluating their performance against rule-based heuristics. The method works for all web operations beyond login verification and produces better results in repair operations than other methods. It produced better results than rule-based heuristics and smaller LLMs because Gemma achieved 53.3% and LLaMA achieved 46.7% success rates. The research evaluates system performance through its assessment of execution time and system resource consumption to demonstrate its suitability for CI/CD operations. It demonstrates how LLMs can decrease maintenance expenses while enhancing testing precision through their upcoming development stages which will concentrate on prompt optimization and domain-specific fine-tuning and industrial-scale deployment.
ER  - 

