AB  - In an era of rapid digital transformation, Artificial Intelligence (AI) ready enterprises, especially in healthcare and financial sectors, must prioritize the protection of sensitive data in development environments to ensure a strong foundation of highly secure applications and data governance. Based on our experience in implementing large-scale programs, we have observed that duplicating production data for testing and development often lacks strong governance, leaving organizations vulnerable to regulatory breaches and substantial penalties under HIPAA, GDPR, and CCPA. Personally identifiable information (PII) and protected health information (PHI) frequently remain unmasked in non-production systems. This paper discusses a persistent gap in data masking policies and introduces a three-stage automation framework: (1) classification of sensitive data based on regulatory standards; (2) seamless integration of automated masking into DevSecOps and CI/CD workflows; and (3) continuous adaptation through AI and human-in-the-loop feedback to maintain compliance and mitigate emerging risks. Using this original and innovative policy-aware framework, we apply data masking across two regulated industries. In healthcare, a clinical trial consent system was enhanced with AI-assisted masking and real-time audit logging, improving HIPAA readiness. In financial services, GenAI utilizes a use case for trading and IPO advisory, embedded masking, telemetry, and real-time GDPR scoring, resulting in a 92% reduction in leakage and an improvement in compliance from 70% to 96%. Together, the validated case studies provide a replicable model for integrating privacy into DevSecOps workflows, enabling secure, automated, and enterprise-scale adoption of AI.
ER  - 

